## Why This Is Asked
Tests a new, well-defined operator (Maj) within Kleene-like three-valued logic and requires concrete CSP reasoning with backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic semantics
- a custom 3-ary Maj operator (majority with T/F bias)
- constraint propagation and backtracking in non-binary domains

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v){ return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function maj(a,b,c){
  const t=[a,b,c].filter(x=>x==='T').length;
  const f=[a,b,c].filter(x=>x==='F').length;
  if(t>=2) return 'T';
  if(f>=2) return 'F';
  return 'U';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend Maj to 4 inputs while preserving monotonicity?
- What is the impact of introducing additional unknowns on backtracking complexity?

## Why This Is Asked
Every candidate demonstrates logic reasoning and basic constraint solving relevant to planning validations in software.

## Key Concepts
- Propositional logic and implications (A→B means ¬A ∨ B)
- Satisfiability with small variable sets
- Backtracking with constraint propagation for pruning

## Code Example
```javascript
function isSatisfiable(constraints, numVars = 3) {
  const vars = Array.from({length: numVars}, (_, i) => String.fromCharCode(65 + i));

  function backtrack(index, assignment) {
    if (index === numVars) return constraints.every(fn => fn(assignment));

    for (const val of [0, 1]) {
      assignment[vars[index]] = val;
      if (constraints.every(fn => fn(assignment))) {
        if (backtrack(index + 1, assignment)) return true;
      }
    }
    return false;
  }

  return backtrack(0, {});
}

const constraints = [
  a => a.A === 0 || a.B === 1,
  a => a.B === 0 || a.C === 1,
  a => a.A === 1 || a.C === 1
];
console.log(isSatisfiable(constraints)); // true
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to thousands of variables with CNF clauses?
- What are the trade-offs of backtracking vs. SAT solvers in practice?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests reasoning with partial information in policy constraints using 3-valued semantics, plus constructing a concrete model and a practical propagation approach.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic semantics for ∧, ∨, ¬, and →
- Evaluate constraints with partial assignments to avoid any F outcomes
- Backtracking and forward propagation tailored to a small constraint set

## Code Example
```javascript
// Lightweight Kleene helpers (conceptual)
const rank = {F:0, U:1, T:2};
const not3 = v => v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U';
const min3 = (a,b) => rank[a] <= rank[b] ? a : b;
const max3 = (a,b) => rank[a] >= rank[b] ? a : b;
const imp3 = (a,b) => max3(not3(a), b);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to detect unsatisfiable sets efficiently?
- How does adding a priority among constraints affect the search strategy?

